/*! \page psfts_thermoint_page  Thermodynamic Integration

\ref psfts_psa_page          (Prev) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref psfts_algorithm_page    (Next) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

Thermodynamic integration is a technique for computing a difference in
values of the free energy in two states characterized by different values
of a parameter. This free energy difference is computed by numerically
integrating the derivative of the free energy \f$ F \f$ with respect to
the relevant model parameter. 

The free energy \f$ F \f$ of a field theoretic model is given by the 
usual relationship
\f[
   F = -\ln Z
\f]
in which \f$ Z \f$ is a field theoretic partition function. Here and
hereafter, we use "thermal" units for energy, in which \f$ kT = 1 \f$.
For a theory that uses a partial saddle-point approximation,
\f[
   Z = \frac{1}{N} \int \! {\cal D}W_{-} \; e^{-H[W_{-},W_{+}^{*}] } \quad,
\f]
where \f$ N \f$ is a constant that is independent of the field 
configuration., Here, we use \f$ W_{-} \f$ to denote a list of the 
\f$ M -1 \f$ fields \f$ W_{1}, \ldots W_{M-1} \f$ that couple to 
composition fluctuations, and \f$ W_{+}^{*} \f$ to denote the 
partial saddle-point configuration of the pressure-like field
\f$ W_{+} \f$$.  Correspondingly, we use \f$ \int {\cal D}W_{-} \f$ 
to denote a functional integral with respect to these \f$ M - 1 \f$ 
field components. 

\section psfts_thermoint_N_sec Normalization constant

PSCF uses a convention for the constant \f$ N \f$ which is given for 
AB systems ( \f$ M = 2 \f$) by
\f[
   N \equiv
   \int {\cal D} W_{-}
   \exp \left \{
   - \int \! d{\bf r} \;
   \frac{ W_{-}^{2}({\bf r}) }{v \chi }
   \right \}
\f]
When approximated by a product of Gaussian integrals on a lattice
containing \f$ G \f$ mesh points in a volume \f$ V \f$, this yields
\f[
   \ln N = \frac{G}{2} 
   \ln  \left ( \frac{\pi \chi v G }{V} \right ) \quad.
\f]
The generalization to the case in which \f$ M > 2 \f$, but in which 
all \f$ M - 1 \f$ nonzero eigenvalues of the projected \f$ \chi \f$
matrix are negative yields a constant
\f[
   \ln N = \frac{G}{2} \sum_{\alpha = 1}^{M-1}
   \ln  \left ( \frac{2\pi |\lambda_{\alpha}| v G  }{ MV } \right ) 
   \quad.
\f]
Here, \f$ \lambda_{1}, \dots, \lambda_{M-1} \f$ denote the first 
\f$ M - 1 \f$ eigenvalues of the projected \f$ \chi \f$ matrix, excluding 
the eigenvalue \f$ \lambda_{M} \f$ that is zero by construction.  

Note that this above convention for \f$ N \f$ yields a value that 
depends on the Flory-Huggins interaction parameters, the monomer 
volume \f$ v \f$, and system volume \f$ V \f$. These dependencies
must be taken into account when computing derivatives of \f$ F \f$ 
with respect to any of these variables.

\section psfts_thermoint_dFdx_sec Free energy derivatives

Suppose \f$ x \f$ is a real-valued parameter that appears in the field 
theoretic Hamiltonian \f$ H \f$ or the above exprssion for \f$ N \f$,
or both. The thermodynamic integration method is based on a 
straightforward theorem that states that the derivative of free 
energy \f$ F \f$ with respect to \f$ x \f$ is given by a sum
\f[
   \frac{\partial F}{\partial x}
   = 
   \left \langle \frac{\partial H}{\partial x} \right \rangle
   + \frac{\partial \ln N}{\partial x}
   \quad.
\f]
Here, \f$ \partial H/\partial x \f$ is a derivative evaluated in a 
fixed field configuration, and \f$ \langle \cdots \rangle \f$ denotes
an equilibrium ensemble average evaluated using a Hamiltonian with the 
specified value of parameter \f$ x \f$. To simplify notation, it is
sometimes useful to express this an average
\f[
   \frac{\partial F}{\partial x} = \langle \Psi \rangle
\f]
in which we define a functional of the fields
\f[
   \Psi = \frac{\partial H}{\partial x} 
   + \frac{\partial \ln N}{\partial x}
\f]
that contains a field-indepdent term arising form the derivative of
\f$ \ln N \f$. 

\section psfts_thermoint_int_sec Numerical integration

The change in \f$ F \f$ associated with a change in \f$ x \f$ from
an initial value \f$ x_{0} \f$ to a final value \f$ x_{1} \f$ is 
obtained by integrating the derivative \f$ \partial F/ \partial x \f$ 
over this range. This sort of integral can be computed numerically by 
either of two methods:

  - **Separate simulations** : Equilibrium simulations can be performed 
    at a set of values of \f$ x \f$ within the range of interest, each 
    of which yields a value of \f$ \partial F/\partial x \f$ at a single 
    value as an ensemble average. The required integral is then 
    approximated by numerical integration of the resulting sequence
    of values.

  - **Continuous integration** : The integral may be computed by using a
    \ref psfts_algo_ramp_page "ramp" to continuously vary the parameter
    of interest over the course of a simulation.

Continuous integration may be used either to compute the change in free
energy associated with a change in a parameter of the standard field
theoretic Hamiltonian (such as an interaction parameter or the monomer
concentration) or as a change in free energy associated with changes 
in the strength of a \ref psfts_algo_perturb_page "perturbation" that 
is added to the standard Hamiltonian to create an unphysical perturbed
Hamiltonian (as done in the Einstein crystal integration method).

